WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.075 --> 00:00:03.576
Samuel: Get down on your knees! Right now!

00:00:05.973 --> 00:00:08.430
When a fugitive does
not want to go to jail,

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:09.540
would they jump?

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:11.670
Yes. I've had several fugitives

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:13.170
that literally jumped off buildings.

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:14.550
Hi, my name is Rasheen Peppers.

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:16.890
I'm a former police captain
with the city of Newark.

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:17.850
10 years of my career

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:20.310
I was detailed to United
States Marshals Service,

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:21.543
tracking down what they consider

00:00:21.543 --> 00:00:23.293
the world's most violent fugitives.

00:00:23.293 --> 00:00:25.710
Today, we'll be looking at fugitive chases

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:29.201
in movies and TVs and see
just how real they are.

00:00:33.528 --> 00:00:37.170
[laughs] 90% of that is just unrealistic.

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:40.110
They're transporting her
without a cage in a car.

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:41.520
That's against protocol.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:43.830
The cage in the car is a partition

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:45.990
that separates the prisoner

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:49.003
from the officers that are driving.

00:00:49.003 --> 00:00:50.013
This protects them.

00:00:50.013 --> 00:00:52.830
In the event the person
does free themselves,

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:55.358
they're unable to get to the
police officers in the front.

00:00:55.358 --> 00:00:58.025
In this scene, where her
being handcuffed to the front,

00:00:58.025 --> 00:00:59.250
that's against protocol.

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:00.570
You're handcuffed to the back.

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:02.580
Because they transported her to the front,

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:05.550
she was able to get to their Tasers.

00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:08.430
If an officer rides in the rear,

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:12.210
they ride with their firearm
away from the prisoner.

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:15.330
So they would never sit
a prisoner to their right

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:17.213
if their weapon was on the right.

00:01:20.002 --> 00:01:22.740
Being transported in the
middle of the convoy,

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:24.030
this can and can't happen.

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:26.940
If there is some type of intelligence

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:31.080
that this person, one,
is extremely dangerous,

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:33.450
two, that there are going to be others

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:37.110
that will be attempting
to free this person,

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:39.000
if you have information like that, yes,

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:41.580
but we would never use marked police cars.

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:43.890
We only transport in unmarked cars.

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:46.380
I rate this one a 2.

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:50.022
Nothing else in this clip
was realistic at all.

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:54.593
I'm here. Where are you?

00:01:54.593 --> 00:01:56.700
So, although Walter, in this scene,

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:58.080
is not a fugitive yet,

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:01.890
it's realistic to bait
a fugitive, if feasible.

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:04.028
One, we actually sent out

00:02:04.028 --> 00:02:06.570
probably close to 100 fugitives.

00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:08.610
We had a mailing list go out

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:11.730
about them winning these prizes.

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:13.738
We had them all come to Atlantic City

00:02:13.738 --> 00:02:15.663
to the convention center.
When they checked in,

00:02:15.663 --> 00:02:19.230
there was a false wall that
you have all the agents behind.

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:21.104
Then, once everything was done,

00:02:21.104 --> 00:02:22.162
[clicks], doors locked,

00:02:22.162 --> 00:02:24.035
and then you have SWAT teams come in,

00:02:24.035 --> 00:02:25.650
and they just start securing everybody.

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:27.465
So, yes, it happens.

00:02:27.465 --> 00:02:30.000
Hank: Walt! Come on out, Walt!

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:31.500
Having a witness there,

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:34.110
I would never have them at a scene.

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:36.450
Our main goal is to protect the public.

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:40.560
So if you have a citizen or
witness that is helping you,

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:43.290
you never want to expose them. Right?

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:47.160
And you wouldn't have two agents

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:50.640
there to do a operation like that,

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:52.560
because it's high risk.

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:55.740
They also responded to a deserted area,

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:58.080
which is not going to happen

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:02.088
unless it was something
that was unplanned.

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:04.950
Hank: Stop.

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:07.380
Lace your fingers behind your head.

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:09.900
Walk backwards to me.

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:11.647
Get on your knees.

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:15.202
By having them turn around
with the hands in the air

00:03:15.202 --> 00:03:18.060
and come towards you
is the right protocol.

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:19.650
When he ordered him to turn around,

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:23.100
they call that a felony
type of apprehension,

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:25.391
because the person may cause

00:03:25.391 --> 00:03:27.810
some type of threat or high risk.

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:30.500
However, the preferable way is

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:33.660
not to really keep him on
his knees with his hands up,

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:35.490
because if he drops his hands,

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:36.900
you can't see them anymore.

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:38.010
And now you have a situation

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:39.630
that he has a firearm in his hand.

00:03:39.630 --> 00:03:42.510
So many of the officers
have them in prone positions

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:44.280
with their hands out to their sides

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:45.630
and their palms facing up.

00:03:45.630 --> 00:03:47.530
I give it a 6 for this scene.

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:55.260
See, right here, a car hit a motorcycle,

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:58.200
and the officers are still chasing Bourne.

00:03:58.200 --> 00:03:59.280
That's just not real.

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:01.745
It's paramount to make
sure that the person

00:04:01.745 --> 00:04:03.600
that was hit on that motorcycle is OK.

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:05.395
And if the person that you pushed

00:04:05.395 --> 00:04:06.870
needs any medical treatment,

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:10.260
you would radio in that the
perpetrator is still on foot,

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:12.501
not continue to chase
Bourne at this point.

00:04:18.340 --> 00:04:19.590
Yeah, it was realistic.

00:04:19.590 --> 00:04:21.600
The officers were in a foot chase.

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:24.270
They don't have access
to a boat at that time.

00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:26.670
So it appeared that
they were waving to see

00:04:26.670 --> 00:04:28.560
if they can get the captain's attention

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:29.393
to let the captain know,

00:04:29.393 --> 00:04:31.020
there's someone that
jumped onto your boat.

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:32.480
However, what should have been done

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:35.220
in the interim was that
they would need to call

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:38.528
whoever governs their water to
advise them to stop the boat.

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:44.250
After he got off the boat,

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:46.170
there was pretty much no eye contact.

00:04:46.170 --> 00:04:48.120
So at that point they should
have been calling out,

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:49.230
trying to set a perimeter.

00:04:49.230 --> 00:04:51.840
It happens to us, not often,

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:54.540
but enough that you find fugitives

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:56.970
that are very athletic, very agile,

00:04:56.970 --> 00:04:59.340
that are able to avoid apprehension.

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:01.080
I remember we had a guy,

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:02.430
he jumps right off the roof,

00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:04.560
he falls onto a iron-cast fence.

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:06.120
He literally takes his arm out,

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:07.620
and pew, he runs.

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:09.000
I rated this one an 8.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:11.217
What color was that ambulance, Renfro?

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:15.510
That scene is very realistic.

00:05:15.510 --> 00:05:17.417
It was quintessential for them to use

00:05:17.417 --> 00:05:19.020
some type of aerial support.

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:21.905
Normally, we know when
we need a helicopter

00:05:21.905 --> 00:05:23.100
is when we plan things

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:25.860
by gathering intelligence,
knowing the terrain,

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:28.590
and include a helicopter if we need it.

00:05:28.590 --> 00:05:31.470
This right here was something
that they were not expecting.

00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:34.290
But what's not shown in
the clip is that this guy,

00:05:34.290 --> 00:05:37.650
he's probably hours ahead of them.

00:05:37.650 --> 00:05:39.555
So when the information came in

00:05:39.555 --> 00:05:41.820
that he was spotted in a ambulance,

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:44.370
they needed the helicopter
to at least gain ground.

00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:46.018
Get on the horn. Bring Biggs and Newman in

00:05:46.018 --> 00:05:48.035
from the south side of that viaduct.

00:05:53.070 --> 00:05:54.930
Well, there's no right or wrong in that.

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:57.570
However, when you're
blocking off a viaduct

00:05:57.570 --> 00:06:00.450
and you're believing you're
chasing a dangerous fugitive,

00:06:00.450 --> 00:06:02.280
now you have locked the citizens

00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:04.500
in there with the perpetrator.

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:08.550
I may have stationed a
vehicle or some officers

00:06:08.550 --> 00:06:10.772
so that each car that's
coming out of the viaduct

00:06:10.772 --> 00:06:12.810
was stopped to make sure he did not jump

00:06:12.810 --> 00:06:14.190
into another vehicle.

00:06:14.190 --> 00:06:17.468
But to completely shut it off,
I would not have done that.

00:06:17.468 --> 00:06:18.934
Put a man in there!

00:06:23.430 --> 00:06:25.470
Have I personally chased
people through a sewer?

00:06:25.470 --> 00:06:29.395
No. But have I chased
them through very high

00:06:29.395 --> 00:06:31.750
and dangerous situations? Yes.

00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:34.050
So I would've did the same
thing the deputy had done.

00:06:34.050 --> 00:06:37.170
I would've had my team
begin to check the vehicles.

00:06:37.170 --> 00:06:39.467
That doesn't mean that we would prolong

00:06:39.467 --> 00:06:40.590
going into the sewer,

00:06:40.590 --> 00:06:42.390
but at least while that's being done,

00:06:42.390 --> 00:06:45.440
we know that the vehicles are
also being checked as well.

00:06:47.159 --> 00:06:49.034
I didn't kill my wife!

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:51.450
I don't care!

00:06:51.450 --> 00:06:53.340
So, that reaction is true.

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:55.680
Many fugitive trackers don't really care

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:57.360
if they did the crime or not.

00:06:57.360 --> 00:06:59.191
Only thing we know is that a warrant

00:06:59.191 --> 00:07:00.990
has been signed for your arrest,

00:07:00.990 --> 00:07:04.726
and our job is to bring you to
that respective jurisdiction.

00:07:04.726 --> 00:07:06.585
Samuel: Put that gun down!

00:07:08.101 --> 00:07:10.290
What the deputy did was actually protocol.

00:07:10.290 --> 00:07:13.023
He was by himself. There
was no reason to engage him,

00:07:13.023 --> 00:07:16.200
because he could have caused
the situation to get worse.

00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:17.640
In a real-life situation,

00:07:17.640 --> 00:07:19.320
had he turned around with that gun,

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:21.300
'cause the command was,
"Put the gun down,"

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:23.310
normally, that would've
been a probable cause

00:07:23.310 --> 00:07:24.385
for use of deadly force.

00:07:28.750 --> 00:07:31.020
When a fugitive does
not want to go to jail

00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:33.661
and he's cornered, would they jump?

00:07:33.661 --> 00:07:35.820
Yes, I've had several fugitives

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:37.350
that literally jumped off buildings.

00:07:37.350 --> 00:07:40.083
He should've got on a
radio, gave his location,

00:07:40.083 --> 00:07:43.590
and then they could have
hopefully taken him into custody.

00:07:43.590 --> 00:07:45.150
But, you know, he jumped.

00:07:45.150 --> 00:07:47.400
I rate this scene 8 out of 10.

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:49.115
Paramedic: 90 seconds.
McKenna: Let's go.

00:07:49.115 --> 00:07:52.156
Wait, wait, wait. It's 1 a.m., not 1 p.m.

00:07:52.156 --> 00:07:53.514
Anything goes wrong, you can blame us.

00:07:53.514 --> 00:07:54.780
Yeah, well something will go wrong,

00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:55.740
and then you're all f---ed.

00:07:55.740 --> 00:07:57.863
So, they're going back and
forth, and that happens.

00:07:57.863 --> 00:08:00.600
Local government working
with federal government

00:08:00.600 --> 00:08:01.590
is very realistic.

00:08:01.590 --> 00:08:05.230
So, you have NYPD here,
you have the FBI here.

00:08:05.230 --> 00:08:07.058
You have the lead detective stating

00:08:07.058 --> 00:08:08.783
what he believes that needs to be done,

00:08:08.783 --> 00:08:10.500
and the FBI has a different position.

00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:12.060
Different agencies,
different personalities,

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:13.440
different methodologies.

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:15.308
Close the island.

00:08:15.308 --> 00:08:16.860
What island?

00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:18.668
Manhattan.

00:08:18.668 --> 00:08:21.720
Arbitrarily lock down the whole island.

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:22.740
It's not realistic.

00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:25.612
That imposes on millions
and millions of people

00:08:25.612 --> 00:08:27.722
trying to move in and out of the city.

00:08:27.722 --> 00:08:28.952
It's unjustified.

00:08:28.952 --> 00:08:31.800
For terrorist attacks,
it may be justified.

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:34.350
However, that would never
come from a NYPD officer.

00:08:34.350 --> 00:08:36.120
That would definitely come from someone

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:37.883
in the federal authorities

00:08:37.883 --> 00:08:40.050
to make a determination of that sort.

00:08:40.050 --> 00:08:41.241
In this scene, they haven't even

00:08:41.241 --> 00:08:43.650
identified who the perpetrators are.

00:08:43.650 --> 00:08:46.260
So who and what are they chasing?

00:08:46.260 --> 00:08:48.510
It's about finding out who did it,

00:08:48.510 --> 00:08:50.512
not locking down Manhattan.

00:08:50.512 --> 00:08:52.485
Catch them by 5.

00:08:52.485 --> 00:08:53.525
Wait, is that a yes or a no?

00:08:53.525 --> 00:08:55.410
Catch them by 5.

00:08:55.410 --> 00:08:57.660
This guy gives Detective Davis

00:08:57.660 --> 00:09:01.320
until 5 a.m. to find the
perpetrators of this crime,

00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:02.970
and it's already 1 a.m.

00:09:02.970 --> 00:09:05.662
Four hours is just not
enough, because, No. 1,

00:09:05.662 --> 00:09:08.100
you're talking about closing
down an island, right?

00:09:08.100 --> 00:09:09.660
That's going to take
them more than four hours

00:09:09.660 --> 00:09:11.160
just to close the island down.

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:14.580
And, No. 2, a deputy mayor
does not have the authority

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:17.490
or possess the acumen when
it comes to law enforcement

00:09:17.490 --> 00:09:19.770
to determine how many hours it would take

00:09:19.770 --> 00:09:22.830
for a detective to solve a crime.

00:09:22.830 --> 00:09:24.030
When you're tracking a fugitive,

00:09:24.030 --> 00:09:27.030
everything is a case-by-case basis.

00:09:27.030 --> 00:09:30.330
It could be from one hour to five years.

00:09:30.330 --> 00:09:33.480
So that's not even ethical
to give him a time frame.

00:09:33.480 --> 00:09:36.330
There are 21 bridges in
and out of Manhattan.

00:09:36.330 --> 00:09:38.130
Shut them down.

00:09:38.130 --> 00:09:39.690
Stop every train that leaves the island

00:09:39.690 --> 00:09:41.010
and loop the subways.

00:09:41.010 --> 00:09:42.600
Cutting off bridges is not

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:45.090
as simple as the movie portrays.

00:09:45.090 --> 00:09:47.640
They don't even have
control over those bridges.

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:49.860
However, if you have, as an officer,

00:09:49.860 --> 00:09:52.950
reasonable belief that
you have a perpetrator

00:09:52.950 --> 00:09:55.350
that's on a specific bridge,

00:09:55.350 --> 00:09:58.470
specific train, or any
type of transportation,

00:09:58.470 --> 00:10:01.350
yes, you have the authority to seize it

00:10:01.350 --> 00:10:03.120
so that you can make the apprehension.

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:05.310
However, if you're going
to get into something big

00:10:05.310 --> 00:10:07.080
and elaborate, like the scene,

00:10:07.080 --> 00:10:09.960
you will have to contact
those governing authorities

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:11.940
to ask permission to do that.

00:10:11.940 --> 00:10:15.150
I gave this a 4 out of 10.

00:10:15.150 --> 00:10:18.350
No detective has the authority

00:10:18.350 --> 00:10:20.525
to shut down a whole city.

00:10:20.525 --> 00:10:22.410
Frank: Hello, Carl.

00:10:22.410 --> 00:10:25.350
Carl: Barry Allen, Secret Service.

00:10:25.350 --> 00:10:27.930
It's definitely something
that does take place.

00:10:27.930 --> 00:10:31.013
Personally, I've received at least 12

00:10:31.013 --> 00:10:32.970
calls from fugitives.

00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:36.420
In this scene, Agent Carl is a FBI agent

00:10:36.420 --> 00:10:39.003
tracking a fugitive.

00:10:39.003 --> 00:10:42.330
FBI, they do have a fugitive unit,

00:10:42.330 --> 00:10:44.780
but the Marshals Service
specializes in it.

00:10:44.780 --> 00:10:48.213
In my suite at the
Stuyvesant Arms, room 3113.

00:10:49.050 --> 00:10:50.520
You're not going to Vegas.

00:10:50.520 --> 00:10:52.020
You're not in the Stuyvesant Arms.

00:10:52.020 --> 00:10:55.860
It's very realistic for
the agent to believe

00:10:55.860 --> 00:10:59.910
that the fugitive was
providing false information.

00:10:59.910 --> 00:11:02.177
Nine times out of 10 when
a fugitive calls you,

00:11:02.177 --> 00:11:04.620
he's not calling you to turn himself in.

00:11:04.620 --> 00:11:06.390
He's playing checkers.

00:11:06.390 --> 00:11:08.910
He's trying to find out what you know

00:11:08.910 --> 00:11:10.860
so he can make his next move.

00:11:10.860 --> 00:11:13.200
I'll tell you what I am sure of.

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:15.425
You're going to get caught.
It's a mathematical fact.

00:11:15.425 --> 00:11:17.808
When the agent tells him
it's a mathematical fact

00:11:17.808 --> 00:11:18.780
that you're going to get caught,

00:11:18.780 --> 00:11:20.147
it's statistically the truth.

00:11:20.147 --> 00:11:24.330
93% to 95% of fugitives are apprehended.

00:11:24.330 --> 00:11:28.110
Movies show that fugitives are
unlikely to be apprehended.

00:11:28.110 --> 00:11:29.670
So it's definitely much higher

00:11:29.670 --> 00:11:33.060
than what the movies and TV shows portray.

00:11:33.060 --> 00:11:35.217
You didn't call just
to apologize, did you?

00:11:35.217 --> 00:11:36.928
You have no one else to call!

00:11:36.928 --> 00:11:38.460
[laughs]

00:11:38.460 --> 00:11:41.340
He was doing exactly what I would've done.

00:11:41.340 --> 00:11:43.740
When you start to attempt
to extract information

00:11:43.740 --> 00:11:44.970
from a fugitive,

00:11:44.970 --> 00:11:47.940
you may tip off to him more what you know.

00:11:47.940 --> 00:11:51.007
